  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Rapid Funding for Lifesaving  WASH Response to Returnees from Sudan</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">ltpgtltbrgtThe project will provide critical WASH services to refugees, returnees, and host communities impacted by the ongoing Sudan crisis, with activities contingent on the activation of specific triggers such as significant increase in the number of refugees crossing borders into South Sudan, or identification of severe gaps in essential services for the newly arriving refugees,&#xA0; to ensure an effective and timely response. Since April 2023, over 970,000 individuals have fled to South Sudan, with significant influxes through 12 main entry points. ltbrgtNCA&#x2019;s focus will center on 2 main entry points in Northern Bhar Ghazal at Kirr Adem and Majokynthiou border crossings due to intensified displacement driven by violence and compounded by severe flooding and a cholera outbreak.ltbrgtTargeting 15,000 individuals (5000 women, 4000 girls, 3500 men, and 2,500 boys)&#xA0; in Aweil East and Aweil West, the project will deliver:ltbrgtConstruction of 8 communal latrine blocks with 4 stances each.ltbrgtRehabilitation of 10 boreholes.ltbrgtInstallation of two SWAT systems.ltbrgtDistribution of WASH NFIs to 15,000 (3000HH) individuals.ltbrgtHygiene promotion activities.ltbrgtAll construction-related activities will follow a NEAT+ assessment to mitigate environmental risks. NCA will collaborate with the Visually Impaired Association (VIA) to ensure accessibility for individuals with disabilities and to reduce protection risks. Coordination with state authorities and the WASH Cluster will align efforts and optimize outcomes.ltbrgtlt/pgt</narrative>
  </description>
